    "q-167067": {
      "dom_id": "question-167067",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What are the $xy$-coordinates of the point $A?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$- and $y$-axes.\n\n- The point is $2$ units to the *left* of the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $x$-axis. So, its $x$-coordinate is $-2.$\n\n- The point is $3$ units *above* the origin, in the *positive* direction of the $y$-axis. So, its $y$-coordinate is $3.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of our point are $(-2,3).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
