    "q-167075": {
      "dom_id": "question-167075",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,-2)$ and is parallel to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ that passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,-2).$ \n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-3) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "",
          "tag": "mathquill",
          "choices": [],
